---
title: "View Subscription"
---

When a server pushes live updates, sending updates for rows the user cannot see is wasted work. The `viewSubscription` grid option tells your application which rows to subscribe to and unsubscribe from as rows enter and leave the view.

View subscription requires the `ViewSubscriptionModule` and [Row IDs](./row-ids/), and is supported by the [Client-Side Row Model](./row-models/#client-side) and the [Server-Side Row Model](./server-side-model/) {% enterpriseIcon /%}.

The example below streams price updates for the subscribed stocks only, over a simulated websocket, and applies them with [Async Transactions](./data-update-high-frequency/).

All examples on this page use the enterprise [Quick Access Toolbar](./toolbar/) {% enterpriseIcon /%} for their controls and counters.

- Scrolling produces subscribe and unsubscribe batches, logged to the console with their row count.
- Clearing **Subscription active** sets `viewSubscriptionActive` to `false`, which unsubscribes every row and stops the stream. Checking it subscribes the rows in view again.

{% gridExampleRunner title="View Subscription with Flat Data" name="flat-view-subscription" exampleHeight=615 /%}

## Handlers

Provide `subscribeRows` and `unsubscribeRows` in `viewSubscription`. When the grid is ready, it calls `subscribeRows` with the rows in view, unless `viewSubscriptionActive` is `false`. It then calls both handlers as rows enter and leave the view.

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    getRowId: (params) => params.data.id,
    viewSubscription: {
        subscribeRows: (rows, params) => {
            stream.subscribe(rows.map((row) => row.id));
        },
        unsubscribeRows: (rows, params) => {
            stream.unsubscribe(rows.map((row) => row.id));
        },
    },
};
```

- `subscribeRows` receives `ViewRow` objects, which include the row `data`. Call `params.api.getRowNode(row.id)` for the row node.
- `unsubscribeRows` receives `ViewRowRef` objects, captured when the row was subscribed, as its row node may no longer exist. They hold the `id`, `type`, `parentKeys`, `level` and, for group and tree data rows, the `route`.
- Every subscribed row is unsubscribed exactly once. When rows change, `unsubscribeRows` is called before `subscribeRows`.
- The handlers are called synchronously, and a returned promise is not awaited. Queue slow work, such as sending the IDs to the server, and return straight away.
- Subscriptions are tracked by row ID. A row whose ID changes, through new row data, a transaction or a refresh, is unsubscribed with its old ID and subscribed with the new one.
- New row data, and a Server-Side Row Model refresh without a purge, keep the rows whose IDs are unchanged. A kept row stays subscribed, even when its data changes. A refresh with `purge: true` unsubscribes every row, then subscribes them again as they reload.
- A row that moves to another parent is unsubscribed with its old `parentKeys` and `route`, then subscribed with the new ones. So is a tree data row that gains its first child or loses its last one, with `hasChildren` updated.

## Row Types

Each row has a `type`:

- `'group'`: a group row, created by [Row Grouping](./grouping/) or [Pivoting](./pivoting/), or loaded as a group by the Server-Side Row Model. The `route` holds the group keys down to and including this row. `data` is `undefined` for group rows the grid creates.
- `'tree'`: a row in [Tree Data](./tree-data/). `hasChildren` is `true` for rows with children. The `route` is set for every row in client-side tree data, and for the expandable rows in server-side tree data.
- `'leaf'`: any other row, such as a row in flat data or a leaf row under groups. It has no `route`.

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    viewSubscription: {
        subscribeRows: (rows) => {
            rows.forEach((row) => {
                if (row.type === 'group') {
                    stream.subscribeToGroup(row.route);
                } else {
                    stream.subscribe(row.id);
                }
            });
        },
        unsubscribeRows: (rows) => {
            rows.forEach((row) => {
                if (row.type === 'group') {
                    stream.unsubscribeFromGroup(row.route);
                } else {
                    stream.unsubscribe(row.id);
                }
            });
        },
    },
};
```

If the stream only has updates for leaf rows, skip group rows and tree data rows that have children in `subscribeRows`, and ignore the IDs you did not subscribe to in `unsubscribeRows`.

### Row Grouping {% enterpriseIcon /%} {% #row-grouping %}

The example below [groups](./grouping/) stocks by sector, and the handlers subscribe the stocks only. Each expanded group row [aggregates](./aggregation/) the largest change and the total volume of its stocks, and updates as the stocks in view stream. To keep a group row current while its stocks are out of view, subscribe to the group row, as in the [Pivoting](#pivoting) example.

- Scrolling, sorting and filtering produce subscribe and unsubscribe batches.
- **New Row Data** gives every stock a new ID, so the old stocks are unsubscribed and the new ones subscribed.

{% gridExampleRunner title="View Subscription with Row Grouping" name="client-side-view-subscription" exampleHeight=615 /%}

### Tree Data {% enterpriseIcon /%} {% #tree-data %}

The example below shows a portfolio of regions, sectors and stocks as [Tree Data](./server-side-model-tree-data/), loaded over a simulated websocket that also streams updates for the subscribed rows. Region and sector rows show the total value of their stocks, which the server streams whenever one of those stocks moves. The updates are applied with [Asynchronous Transactions](./server-side-model-updating-transactions/#asynchronous-updates), using each row's `parentKeys` as the route.

- Scrolling, expanding groups and loading blocks produce subscribe and unsubscribe batches.
- **Purge** reloads all rows. The old rows are unsubscribed, and the new rows subscribed as they load.
- With **Subscription active** cleared, rows still load over the socket, but no updates stream.

{% gridExampleRunner title="View Subscription with Server-Side Tree Data" name="server-side-view-subscription" exampleHeight=615 /%}

## Lifecycle

The subscription starts when the grid is ready, unless `viewSubscriptionActive` is `false`.

- Setting `viewSubscriptionActive` to `false` unsubscribes every row and keeps the handlers configured, for example while the grid is in an inactive tab. Setting it back to `true` subscribes the rows in view.
- Setting `viewSubscription` to `undefined`, or destroying the grid, unsubscribes every row. On destroy, the API is still usable in the handlers.
- The handlers are read on each call, so new handlers take effect without unsubscribing any rows. Rows subscribed by the old handlers are unsubscribed through the new ones.

Bind `viewSubscriptionActive` to application state while the handlers stay as they are:

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    viewSubscription: {
        subscribeRows: (rows) => stream.subscribe(rows.map((row) => row.id)),
        unsubscribeRows: (rows) => stream.unsubscribe(rows.map((row) => row.id)),
    },
    viewSubscriptionActive: !document.hidden,
};

document.addEventListener('visibilitychange', () => {
    api.setGridOption('viewSubscriptionActive', !document.hidden);
});
```

If the server loses the subscriptions, for example after a reconnect, re-send the row IDs you track, or set `viewSubscriptionActive` to `false` and back to `true` to subscribe the rows in view again.

## Excluded Rows

Only loaded rows in view are passed to the handlers. These rows are never passed:

- [Loading placeholder rows](./server-side-model-loading-rows/) and [rows that failed to load](./server-side-model-retry/).
- [Detail Rows](./master-detail/) and [Full Width Rows](./full-width-rows/).
- [Group total (footer) rows and the grand total row](./aggregation-total-rows/), pinned or not, and the root row shown in [pivot mode](./pivoting/) without row groups.
- Rows [provided](./row-pinning/#providing-pinned-row-data) with `pinnedTopRowData` or `pinnedBottomRowData`, which have no source row in the row model.

Rows [pinned](./row-pinning/) by the user or with `isRowPinned` are always in view, so they stay subscribed while their place in the grid is scrolled away. They are unsubscribed once they are neither pinned nor in view.

Sticky group rows are unsubscribed once their row scrolls above the view, even while they stay stuck to the top of the grid.

[Print layout](./printing/) renders every row, so no rows are subscribed while `domLayout` is `'print'`. Switching to it unsubscribes every row, and switching back subscribes the rows in view.

With `domLayout: 'autoHeight'`, the grid is as tall as all of its rows and the page scrolls, so every row counts as in view and is subscribed. Keep the normal layout for grids with many rows.

## Debouncing

The grid calls the handlers once the view has stopped moving for `debounceMs` milliseconds. Rows scrolled past quickly are never subscribed, and slow handlers cause one short pause once scrolling stops, with no lag while scrolling.

- Raise `debounceMs` when each subscription is costly.
- Lower it, down to `0`, to subscribe sooner. The handlers then run while scrolling, both possibly in the same frame, so keep each call under about 5 ms or scrolling lags.
- Data changes, such as transactions, do not push a pending call back.

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    viewSubscription: {
        subscribeRows: (rows) => stream.subscribe(rows.map((row) => row.id)),
        unsubscribeRows: (rows) => stream.unsubscribe(rows.map((row) => row.id)),
        debounceMs: 150,
    },
};
```

## Pivoting

In [pivot mode](./pivoting/), each row in view is a group row, passed with `type: 'group'`. Each of its cells aggregates the leaf rows under it for one pivot result column. A subscription covers the whole row, including result columns scrolled out of view, so the server sends updates for everything the row aggregates.

### Client-Side Row Model

Group rows keep their IDs when pivot mode or the pivot columns change, so only rows that move into or out of view are subscribed or unsubscribed.

The example below pivots stocks by exchange, grouped by sector, and shows the largest change and the total volume for each exchange. The grid aggregates the group rows itself, so for each subscribed group row, the handlers send its `route` and the grouped fields, and the server streams every stock in the group.

The application decides which data a subscribed row needs. Here a stream runs only when the selected columns show its data: a stock streams while its price, change or volume is visible, and the stocks of a group stream while the group row shows an aggregate. The example listens to the `columnValueChanged`, `columnVisible` and `columnPivotModeChanged` events to pause and resume the streams.

- The grid aggregates each group row from its stocks, so the five sector rows stream all 100 stocks, and **Streamed stocks** is much larger than **Subscribed rows**.
- Removing every value column in the [Columns Tool Panel](./tool-panel-columns/) drops **Streamed stocks** to 0, while the group rows stay subscribed.
- With pivot mode off, hiding **Price**, **Change %** and **Volume** does the same. Showing **Price** streams only the stocks in view, as group rows leave it blank.
- Changing the row groups or the pivot columns changes which rows are subscribed.
- **New Row Data** gives every stock a new ID.

{% gridExampleRunner title="View Subscription with Pivoting" name="pivot-view-subscription" exampleHeight=615 /%}

### Server-Side Row Model

Turning pivot mode on or off, or changing the pivot columns, reloads the rows. Every row is unsubscribed, and the reloaded rows are subscribed as their blocks load.

The example below has the same stocks, controls and pausing rules as the [Client-Side Row Model example](#client-side-row-model), with these differences:

- The server groups, pivots and aggregates the stocks, and sends the pivot result fields with each block, so the grid creates the pivot result columns. `getRowId` is called for group rows too, and gives them an ID from their route.
- The server sends each subscribed group row whole, recomputed whenever one of its stocks moves, so **Streamed rows** equals **Subscribed rows** while no stream is paused.
- The updates are applied with [Asynchronous Transactions](./server-side-model-updating-transactions/#asynchronous-updates), using each row's `parentKeys` as the route.

{% gridExampleRunner title="View Subscription with Server-Side Pivoting" name="ssrm-pivot-view-subscription" exampleHeight=615 /%}

